# Highly Efficient and Reliable Semitransparent Perovskite Solar Cells via Top Electrode Engineering

## abstract
Transparent electrodes are essential to allow optical transparency for realizing semitransparent perovskite solar cells (ST‐PSCs). This study addresses gallium‐ and titanium‐doped indium oxide (IO:GT) between the electron transport layer (ETL) and top electrode to potentially replace conventional indium tin oxide (ITO) used in inverted ST‐PSCs. The shallower work function (−4.23 eV) of IO:GT than that (−4.69 eV) of conventional ITO contributes to suppressing the formation of the Schottky barrier and enhancing the charge transport at the ETL/cathode interface. By adopting IO:GT, the ST‐PSC exhibits an enhancement in power conversion efficiency (PCE) from 8.59% to 17.90% (certified 17.53%) with an average visible transmittance (AVT) of 21.9%, which is the record PCE at similar AVT among all ST‐PSCs reported to date. Moreover, combining these ST‐PSCs as the top cell, a four‐terminal perovskite–perovskite tandem solar cell is realized, showing a high PCE of 23.35%. Furthermore, the stability of the ST‐PSCs is confirmed excellent, maintaining over 96% of the initial PCE after 1864 h (≈77 days) in air ambient without encapsulation, which is better than the device employing a metal cathode. Therefore, these results demonstrate that the adoption of IO:GT can be a promising route for efficient and stable inverted ST‐PSCs with preferred transparency.

Transparent electrodes are essential to allow optical transparency for realizing semitransparent perovskite solar cells (ST-PSCs). This study addresses gallium- and titanium-doped indium oxide (IO:GT) between the electron transport layer (ETL) and top electrode to potentially replace conventional indium tin oxide (ITO) used in inverted ST-PSCs. The shallower work function $(-4.23\ e V)$ of IO:GT than that (−4.69 eV) of conventional ITO contributes to suppressing the formation of the Schottky barrier and enhancing the charge transport at the ETL/cathode interface. By adopting IO:GT, the ST-PSC exhibits an enhancement in power conversion efficiency (PCE) from $8.59\%$ to $17.90\%$ (certified $17.53\%$ ) with an average visible transmittance (AVT) of $21.9\%$ , which is the record PCE at similar AVT among all ST-PSCs reported to date. Moreover, combining these ST-PSCs as the top cell, a four-terminal perovskite–perovskite tandem solar cell is realized, showing a high PCE of $23.35\%$ . Furthermore, the stability of the ST-PSCs is confirmed excellent, maintaining over $96\%$ of the initial PCE after $\mathsf{1864h1approx77}$ days) in air ambient without encapsulation, which is better than the device employing a metal cathode. Therefore, these results demonstrate that the adoption of IO:GT can be a promising route for efficient and stable inverted ST-PSCs with preferred transparency.
## 1. Introduction
Semitransparent perovskite solar cells (ST-PSCs) have attracted considerable attention due to their increased potential for building-integrated photovoltaics and tandem solar cells.[1] STPSCs typically exhibit lower power conversion efficiency (PCE) values than opaque inorganic–organic hybrid perovskite solar cells (PSCs) due to the loss of photocurrent by employing transparent electrodes. Also, transparent conductive oxides (TCOs) generally have less conductivity than opaque metals, such as Au and Ag. Hence, oxide–metal–oxide multilayer structures have been studied to overcome the conductivity issue. However, the process cost and time increase due to the required repetition of vacuum deposition steps. Therefore, a simpler and more effective method can be the single-step deposition of TCO with suitable properties for the transparent top electrode of ST-PSCs. The conventionally used TCO is indium tin oxide (ITO), which has excellent stability, transmittance, and low sheet resistance. However, preparing ITO with such desirable properties requires a hightemperature annealing process for crystallization. To avoid thermal damage to underlying perovskites, including organic charge transport layers, ambient temperature deposition of ITO is necessary, which makes the amorphous phase have poor optoelectronic properties than the crystalline phases. Therefore, there have been many efforts to enhance the properties of amorphous ITO by additional doping.[2] Besides optical and electrical properties, such as transmittance and conductivity, the work function is also important for efficient carrier collection in ST-PSCs. Most of the n–i–p-structured PSCs mainly use Au $(-5.1\mathrm{eV})$ as a deep work function metal electrode for energy band matching with hole-transporting materials,[3] and the inverted p–i–n-structured PSCs use Ag $(-4.5\mathrm{eV})$ and Al $(-4.3\ \mathrm{eV})$ as a shallow work function metal electrode for the electron-transporting layers.[4] If the work function matching between the charge transport layer and the metal electrode is inappropriate, undesirable Schottky contact might occur, limiting the effective charge collection and/ or transport. To prevent the Schottky barrier, buffer layers have been studied in opaque PSCs.[5] Because the amorphous ITO as a top cathode is insufficient in terms of conductivity and work function for ST-PSCs, herein, we propose a shallow work function TCO, Ga- and Ti-doped $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ (IO:GT) electrode, which has enhanced transmittance and conductivity despite the film deposition at ambient temperature $(\approx25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ . Additionally, the work function of the fabricated IO:GT was adjusted as $-4.23\mathrm{eV},$ which is suitable for the inverted-structured ST-PSCs. When the inverted ST-PSCs were manufactured using IO:GT as transparent electrodes for the top cathode, the Schottky barrier effect shown in the ITO-based cell could be suppressed. The top cathode engineering by favorable work function alignment significantly improves the fill factor and considerably increases the PCE from $8.59\%$ (ITO) to $17.90\%$ (IO:GT) with average visible transmittances (AVTs) exceeding $21\%$ . The high-performance ST-PSC was employed as a top cell for four-terminal perovskite tandem solar cells (4T-PTSCs), which provided a remarkable PCE of $23.35\%$ . As for the ambient stability, additionally, the opaque PSCs with conventional $\mathsf{A g}$ electrode revealed PCE reduction exceeding $80\%$ after about a month, whereas ST-PSCs with IO:GT maintained $96\%$ of its initial PCE after $1864\mathrm{~h~}$ $(\approx77$ days), demonstrating superior stability in the atmospheric environment without encapsulation. These impressive results could generate an effective pathway to further improve the performance and stability of ST-PSCs.
## 2. Results and Discussion
## 2.1. Optical and Electrical Characterization of TCO Thin Films
As for a top electrode, basic optoelectronic properties, such as transmission and sheet resistance, are quite important for transparent electronic devices. Figure 1a shows the optical transmittance and electrical properties of amorphous ITO and IO:GT thin films. As developed recently in our group, IO:GT has low oxygen vacancies due to the strong lewis-acid strength of the Ti-dopant, resulting in higher carrier mobility and optical transmission than ITO.[6] In accordance with the previous study,[7] the elements weight ratio of the IO:GT film was Ga $0.4\ \mathrm{wt}.\%$ , Ti $3.4\ \mathrm{wt.}\%$ , and In $96.2\%$ , which were confirmed through X-ray photoemission spectroscopy. Except for those properties, the work functions of the TCO materials at the top electrode are crucial for operating the device. To experimentally assess the ITO and IO:GT work functions, ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) was introduced. The work functions of the ITO and IO:GT films were extracted to $-4.69$ and $-4.23\ \mathrm{eV},$ respectively (Figure  1b). Figure S1 (Supporting Information) shows the raw UPS spectra. The influence of the work function of the top cathode on the energy band alignment is shown in Figure 1c,d. The work function of ITO has a deeper energy level of $-4.69\ \mathrm{eV}$ than the Fermi level of the electron transport layer (ETL) and buffer layer. After contact, the Fermi level of the ETL and buffer layer will align to the highest occupied electron energy of the top cathode. Simultaneously, band bending formed in the ETL, and the buffer layer formed a Schottky barrier that hampers the electron transport. At this time, the accumulated charge carriers may lead to loss due to recombination.[5] However, in the case of IO:GT (Figure 1d), the favorable energy band alignment for charge collection is created due to the shallow work function, indicating that the charge carriers transport of IO:GT used in the top cathode for ST-PSCs could occur efficiently.
## 2.2. Applications of Inverted ST-PSCs
To investigate the effect of the shallow work function IO:GT on the performance of the ST-PSCs, $\mathrm{FA_{0.87}C s_{0.13}P b(I_{0.87}B r_{0.13})_{3}}.$ based solar cells were fabricated with the inverted architec­ture of $\mathrm{ITO/NiO}_{x}/\mathrm{FA}_{0.87}\mathrm{Cs}_{0.13}\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{I}_{0.87}\mathrm{Br}_{0.13})_{3}/\mathrm{PCBM}/\mathrm{BCP}/\mathrm{c}$ cathode (Figure 2a). The opaque PSCs are manufactured when $\mathrm{Ag}$ is deposited as a cathode on BCP, and ST-PSCs are fabricated when a transparent electrode is deposited. The cross-sectional field emission-scanning electron microscopy (FE-SEM) images of the ST-PSCs are presented in Figure  2b, showing an $\mathrm{NiO}_{x}$ $(30~\mathrm{nm})$ hole transport layer (HTL) on a commercial ITO substrate $(10\Omega\bigtriangledown^{-1})$ , a $330\ \mathrm{nm}$ -thick perovskite layer, a PCBM $(30\ \mathrm{nm})$ ) ETL, and a BCP buffer layer. In this structure, it is worthwhile to discuss possible plasma damage by the sputtering process, which was verified using X-ray diffraction and field emission transmission electron microscope (Figures S2 and S3, Supporting Information). There might be some impact on BCP and PCBM because of the sputtering process. Hence, we have made significant efforts to analyze it, however it was too difficult to find and secure any meaningful signal possibly because of those too thin, organic layers. Therefore, we have expanded our analysis to underlying perovskite layer because such high plasma damage is prone to propagate to the underlying organic/inorganic hybrid perovskite layer via very thin organic layers. Regarding the ITO preparation, the sputtering power was lowered from 300 to $100~\mathrm{\mathbb{W}}$ , and $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ produced by the degradation of perovskite due to sputtering damage disappeared. There was also no detectable degradation signal in the case of the cell with IO:GT. The optimized data for the thickness of IO:GT with $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ were shown in Figure S4 and Table S1 (Supporting Information). To analyze the solar cell transparency differences according to the electrode type, the optical transmittance was measured. As shown in Figure  2c, opaque PSCs show almost zero transmittance in all wavelength regions. However, both ST-PSCs that apply either ITO and IO:GT top cathode exhibit AVT values of $21.6\%$ and $21.9\%$ , respectively, and noticeably high transmittance of ${\approx}80\%$ in the near-infrared (NIR) region. Both ST-PSCs transmitted several photons in the $500{-}1000~\mathrm{nm}$ wavelength through ST-PSC, by merit of the high transmittance of both ITO and IO:GT. The $J{-}V$ characteristics of the Ag opaque PSCs and ST-PSCs are shown in Figure 2d, and their champion solar cell parameters are summarized in Table 1. The average parameter values of the measured devices are summarized in Table S2 (Supporting Information). The ST-PSCs with the ITO top cathode exhibited a PCE of $8.59\%$ with an open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{OC}})$ of $1.02\mathrm{V},$ a short-circuit current density $(J_{\mathrm{SC}})$ of $18.27~\mathrm{mA~cm}^{-2}$ , and a fill factor (FF) of $46.1\%$ . In contrast, the ST-PSCs with the IO:GT top cathode exhibited photovoltaic parameters, such as $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $1.12~\mathrm{V},$ $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of $19.28~\mathrm{mA~cm}^{-2}$ , and FF of $82.9\%$ , resulting in a
PCE value of $17.90\%$ . To confirm the performance officially, we certified PV parameters that reveal the PCE of $17.53\%$ as displayed in Figure S5 (Supporting Information). The ST-PSC with ITO had a charge accumulation interface because of the Schottky barrier formed at the BCP–ITO interface, which resulted in an s-shape kink curve in $J{-}V$ characteristics, that led to a poor FF. However, the IO:GT device revealed negligible charge transportation problems due to the shallow work function of IO:GT, and thus its FF value could be remarkably enhanced to $82.9\%$ , thereby increasing the PCE compared to ITO $(8.59\%\rightarrow17.90\%$ ). Meanwhile, there was no hysteresis in ST-PSCs with ITO and IO:GT. The ST-PSCs with ITO exhibited no hysteresis despite the accumulation of charges because the common hysteresis was dominated by the asymmetric mobility of the charge transport layers or migration of ions to the grain boundaries.[8] Furthermore, IO:GT-based ST-PSCs attained noteworthy AVT of $21.9\%$ and comparable performance to opaque PSCs with Ag electrode ( $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of 1.15 V, $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of $21.53\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , FF of $80.2\%$ , and PCE of $19.86\%)$ . The slightly higher $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ values of the opaque PSCs were investigated by EQE measurements. The integrated $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ obtained from the EQE measurements were 20.81 and $19.02\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ for Ag opaque PSCs and IO:GT-based ST-PSCs, respectively (Figure S6, Supporting Information), which agree excellently with the $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ values acquired from the $J{-}V$ curves. Note that the EQE spectrum of the IO:GT-based ST-PSCs was lower than that of the opaque PSCs in the wavelength range of $550{-}750\ \mathrm{nm}$ because of the reduction in the rear reflection from the transparent electrode compared to opaque $\mathrm{Ag.^{[2c,6,9]}}$ Nevertheless, due to the significantly high AVT and PCE, our IO:GT-based ST-PSCs are cutting edge in the field of transparent solar cells.
To investigate the charge accumulation of ST-PSCs, the capacitance of the devices versus the bias voltage was measured. As shown in Figure 3a,b, the capacitance values of the ST-PSCs were measured under 1-sun condition (illumination) and dark conditions to observe the behavior of the excited charge carriers in the devices. Regardless of the electrode type, the capacitance of all conditions increased from near $0.8\mathrm{~V~}$ due to the depletion region of the geometric thickness of the devices.[10] However, the capacitance values of the ITO-based ST-PSCs when under the light increased significantly, and that was divided by two cases: (i) the increase in the whole bias voltage range and (ii) the peak near $0.5~\mathrm{V}.$ Case (i) is the capacitance caused by accumulating excited charge carriers by photons in the device at the BCP–ITO interface. In the case of (ii), while the bias was applied from $-0.2$ to $0.5~\mathrm{V},$ charge carriers are dominant and accumulate, increasing the capacitance. However, after $0.5~\mathrm{V},$ due to the injection of high potential electrons from the cathode (ITO) side, accumulated charge carriers at the BCP–ITO interface were pushed inside, and the capacitance decreased. However, IO:GT demonstrated its excellent charge-transporting ability by exhibiting constant capacitance despite the charge excitation even under illumination. Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements were performed to verify our hypothesis about the behavior of charge carriers. As shown in Figure 3c, the Nyquist plots of ITO- and IO:GT-based ST-PSCs show a distinctive difference in impedance. The $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ values of the IO:GT-based ST-PSCs, which are five times larger than those of the cells using the ITO top cathode, indicate the possibility of recombination (described as the yellow arrow in Figure 1c) has been drastically reduced. The $R_{\mathrm{s}}$ and $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ values of the ST-PSCs with ITO and IO:GT are presented in Figure S7 and Table S3 (Supporting Information). Figure 3d displays the extended $C-V$ curves that directly compare the built-in potential of the devices by the Mott–Schottky analysis. It is found that that device with the IO:GT cathode exhibits the higher built-in potential of $0.90{\mathrm{~V~}}$ compared to the $0.77\mathrm{V}$ of the ITObased ST-PSC, in agreement with the favorable energy band alignment with a shallow work function of IO:GT. Figure  3e manifests that the transient photocurrent (TPC) decay time of the device with IO:GT $(1.06~\upmu\mathrm{s})$ is quicker than ITO $(2.43~\upmu\mathrm{s})$ , implying that the IO:GT based ST-PSC exhibit better charge carrier extraction capability than the ITO based ST-PSC. This phenomenon can be credited to the modified energy band to a shallow work function by IO:GT, which consequently decreased the interface charge accumulation at IO:GT to ETL. Figure  3f shows a light power density (I) dependent $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of the ST-PSCs with ITO and IO:GT. The $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of photovoltaic is proportional to light intensity $(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\propto\mathrm{I}^{\alpha})$ . Here, the slope of the curve is $\alpha.$ The lower the $\alpha,$ the more charges are accumulated at ETL to cathode. We obtained $\alpha=0.93$ for the ST-PSC with IO:GT and $\alpha=0.87$ for the ITO, which suggests that the IO:GT based ST-PSC exhibited a negligible charge accumulation compared to that of ITO. These results indicate that the introduction of IO:GT suppressed the Schottky barrier. Figure S8 (Supporting Information) shows a time-resolved photoluminescence (TR-PL) for ITO and IO:GT-based ST-PSCs. The TR-PL spectra demonstrated that the photoluminescence lifetime gradually decreased from ITO to IO:GT. The ST-PSC with ITO had an average lifetime of $23.5~\mathrm{ns}$ , whereas with IO:GT cathode it had a shorter lifetime of 5.77  ns. This result indicates that the electron extraction and transportation ability of the IO:GT is significantly enhanced, which is due to the improved conductivity, electron mobility, and work function. Figure S9 (Supporting Information) shows the bode-plots of the ST-PSCs with the different translucent electrodes. The curve peak of the spectrum can be used to determine the electron relaxation time in the devices (according to the equation $\tau_{\mathrm{n}}=1/(2\pi\omega_{\mathrm{min}}))$ . It is clear that the electron relaxation time for the ST-PSC with the IO:GT electrode, $6.34~\upmu\mathrm{s}$ , is shorter than the $10.04~{\upmu\mathrm{s}}$ electron relaxation time for the ITO-based ST-PSC. Thus, employment of the IO:GT cathode improves the charge transporting. The above characterizations confirmed that the shallow work function of IO:GT can effectively prevent the Schottky contact in the inverted ST-PSCs and suppress the accumulation of charges, consequently resulting in high-device performance.
## 2.3. Perovskite–Perovskite Four-Terminal Tandem Solar Cells
As previously described, outstanding single-junction inverted ST-PSCs were produced using the IO:GT electrode as the top cathode. Furthermore, the possibility of the transparent cells with the IO:GT electrode to be used as top cells for the 4T-PTSCs with low-bandgap bottom cells were also considered. In the case of 4T-PTSCs, the AVT of the top cell also should be considered to manage the overall performance of the 4T-PTSC. Therefore, the ST-PSCs were systematically tuned by adjusting the bandgap and thickness of the perovskites to increase the AVT in the wavelength region of $400{-}800~\mathrm{nm}$ . The bandgap of each perovskite was verified at the Tauc-plot and the EQE inflection (Figure S10, Supporting Information), and the thickness was monitored using FE-SEM images (Figure S11, Supporting Information). Figure 4a showed the transmittance spectra of various ST-PSCs with different bandgaps and absorber thicknesses, where their AVTs were also included. The reference condition $(1.63~\mathrm{eV},~330~\mathrm{nm}$ ) exhibited an AVT of $21.9\%$ , and the thinner absorber $(1.63~\mathrm{eV},250~\mathrm{nm})$ could deliver the improved AVT of $28.6\%$ due to the increase in transmittance in the wavelength region of $450{\mathrm{-}}750\ \mathrm{\nm}$ . Also, additional wide-bandgap conditions $(1.75\ \mathrm{eV},\ 210\ \mathrm{nm})$ would generate a much-enhanced AVT of $35.3\%$ with the transmittance increase by the blue-shifting absorption edge. However, as the transmittance increased, the light absorbed by the perovskite active layer decreased relatively, leading to lower $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ and PCE of devices. Consequently, as AVT increases, the $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of the device decreases from 19.28 to $15.11\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , and the PCE decreases from 17.90 to $13.17\%$ (Figure $\boldsymbol{4\mathrm{b}}$ and Table 2). As displayed in Figure S12 (Supporting Information), we could clarify it by observing the
EQE and AVT relation. When we designed to reinforce light absorption and photocurrent generation in our devices, EQE significantly increases with the sacrifice of the AVT. Also, in order to allow for providing optical transmission (i.e., the AVT increase $(21.9\%\rightarrow28.6\%\rightarrow35.3\%)$ ), relatively lower EQE had to be obtained. Generally, the optimization of ST-PSCs is evaluated using light use efficiency $(\mathrm{LUE}=\mathrm{AVT}\times\eta\times100)$ .[11] However, the AVT value is more influential than the PCE value because AVT is more likely to have higher values than PCE. Therefore, we suggest the PCE priority LUE $(\mathrm{PLUE}=\mathrm{AVT}\times$ $\eta^{2}\times100)$ value, which yields a figure of merit for ST-PSCs. The $\mathrm{AVT}_{35.3}$ device exhibits the highest LUE of 4.65, which is unprecedented compared to previous studies. Also, the $\mathrm{AVT}_{21.9}$ and $\mathrm{AVT}_{28.6}$ devices showed good performances; however, the LUE values are 3.92 and 4.34, respectively, which are slightly moderate than the best record. In contrast, the PLUE value of $\mathrm{AVT}_{21.9}$ is 0.70, which exceeds those of $\mathrm{AVT}_{28.6}$ (0.66), $\mathrm{AVT}_{35.3}$ (0.61), and any other reported ST-PSC. PLUE value focuses on harvesting rather than transmission of photons compared to the LUE value. As presented in Figure $4\mathrm{c}$ , the PCE-AVT plot compares our ST-PSCs performance with previous studies in various transparent electrodes. The results obtained herein demonstrate record LUE and PLUE values, indicating that they are the best performing devices recorded to date in the field of ST-PSCs. Moreover, we evaluated that ST-PSCs are suitable for use as top cells of 4T-PTSCs based on ${\approx}85\%$ transmittance in the NIR region $(800-1000\ \mathrm{nm})$ ) wavelength (Figure $4\mathrm{a}$ ). Hence, PSCs with a low bandgap of $1.27~\mathrm{eV}$ were prepared and used for 4T-PTSCs with ST-PSCs, and the $J{-}V$ characteristics of the devices are shown in Figure 5a. The filtered low- $E\underline{{g}}$ bottom cells exhibit PCE values of $5.45\%$ , $6.24\%$ , and $7.26\%$ when filtered with $\mathrm{AVT}_{21.9}$ , $\mathrm{AVT}_{28.6}.$ , and $\mathrm{AVT}_{35.3}$ , respectively. As the AVT of the top cell increases, the PCE gradually enhances as the $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of the bottom cell improves. The optimized PCE of the 4T-PTSC was obtained as $23.35\%$ when $\mathrm{AVT}_{21.9}$ was applied as a filter. As shown in Figure 5b, the EQE spectra determine the photocurrent of the 4T-PTSCs according to the condition of top cells. The top cell in 4T-PTSCs absorbs as many photons as possible in the visible light region to produce photocurrent, and allows high transmission in the NIR region so that the bottom cell can obtain more photocurrent. In this perspective, among the top cells with various AVTs, the $\mathrm{AVT}_{21.9}$ cell could only reveal the EQE in the wavelength region of $350{\mathrm{-}}600\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which is obviously higher than the unfiltered low-Eg PSC. The integrated $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ value extracted from EQE was verified; only the 4T-PTSC with $\mathrm{AVT}_{21.9}$ $(29.19\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}},$ was higher than the unfiltered low-Eg PSCs of $29.03\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ (Figure S13a–d, Supporting Information). Therefore, to evaluate the appropriate ST-PSCs in the top cell of the 4T-PTSCs, it is also speculated that PLUE is advantageous to determine whether the photon to be absorbed is reliably generated by the photocurrent.
## 2.4. Investigation of Device Stability
The long-term stability of perovskite-based devices is crucial for the commercialization of PSCs. To compare the stability of solar cells with Ag cathode and IO:GT, unencapsulated inverted opaque PSCs and ST-PSCs were stored in the atmosphere, and their long-term stability was verified. Figure 6a shows the stabilized power output (SPO) graphs of the best performing PSCs with opaque Ag cathode and ST-PSCs with transparent IO:GT cathode. Highly stable power output for $10\mathrm{min}$ was confirmed, indicating the stable operation in 1-sun condition. The histogram for the PCE statistics of 10 devices of each type in Figure 6b demonstrates the reproducibility compared with each electrode. Additionally, the average parameters and the $J{-}V$ curves of the 10 devices of each electrode condition are summarized in Table S1 and Figure S14a–f (Supporting Information). Regardless of the electrode type, the devices exhibited good reproducibility. The long-term stability parameters $\begin{array}{r}{\langle V_{\mathrm{OC}},\ J_{\mathrm{SC}},}\end{array}$ FF, and PCE) of opaque PSCs and ST-PSCs without encapsulation are shown in Figure $\mathsf{S15a-c}$ (Supporting Information) and Figure 6c. The devices were stored and measured in the atmosphere with a relative humidity of $40\mathrm{-}60\%$ . Figure 6c shows that both opaque PSCs and ST-PSCs depicted a slight decrease in the initial PCE until ${\approx}500\mathrm{~h~}$ $(\Delta_{\mathrm{PCE}}<0.2\%)$ . However, after about $600\mathrm{~h~}$ , it was observed that the FF of the opaque PSCs sharply decreased (Figure S14c, Supporting Information). The significant loss of FF can be attributed to AgI formation by iodine ion migration in the ambient atmosphere, which reduces conductivity and thus stability.[12] Therefore, a severe PCE reduction of ${\approx}80\%$ was revealed in the PSC with the Ag electrode. In contrast, our ST-PSC with IO:GT shows excellent long-term stability in the atmosphere with negligible damages by maintaining $96\%$ of the initial PCE when measured after $1864\mathrm{~h~}$ . Even though the initial performance of the Ag-based opaque cell was high $(>19\%)$ , the final PCE after a long time of aging was much more impressive in the case of the transparent device employing the suggested IO:GT electrode. In addition, the heating temperature was treated for $56\mathrm{~h~}$ up to $110^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in a nitrogen glove box to confirm thermal stability (Figure S16, Supporting Information). The devices endured thermal damage, maintaining more than $90\%$ of the initial PCE up to $32\mathrm{~h~}$ . However, after $52\mathrm{~h~}$ , the $\operatorname{Ag}$ device revealed the PCE reduction of about $55\%$ of the initial PCE, on the other hand, the IO:GT-based transparent PV device displayed only $25\%$ reduction. Based on the experimental results, top TCO engineering can boost the overall performance and stability of ST-PSCs, and the proposed IO:GT can be an effective pathway to further contribute to the commercialization of ST-PSC in the future.
## 3. Conclusion
IO:GT transparent top cathode was suggested instead of conventional ITO for a shallow work function and favorable energy band alignment in inverted perovskite solar cells. By suppressing the Schottky barrier at ETL and top electrode, muchenhanced charge collection properties could be demonstrated by EIS and $C-V$ analyses. The IO:GT-based ST-PSCs showed a
PCE of $17.90\%$ , much higher than that of ITO-based ST-PSCs $(8.59\%)$ due to the remarkable enhancement in FF. Based on the top cathode engineering, PLUE of 0.70 with AVT of $21.9\%$ and PCE of $17.90\%$ were obtained, which are better than the performances of other ST-PSCs reported to date. Benefiting from the electrode properties, the AVT values of ST-PSCs were increased from 21.9 to $35.3\%$ , and were combined with lowEg PSCs for 4T-PTSCs, where the best achieved efficiency of 4T-PTSCs was $23.35\%$ , with $\mathrm{AVT}_{21.9}$ as the filter. Furthermore, the impressive long-term environmental stability was attained by employing the IO:GT cathode compared to the metal electrode, which generates a new guideline for essentially adopting the top cathode of the translucent photovoltaic cells for overall performance and stability.
## 4. Experimental Section
Materials: All materials were used as received from commercial companies without any purification. Nickel(II) nitrate hexahydrate $({\mathsf{N i}}({\mathsf{N O}}_{3})_{2}$ $6{\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}$ , $99.999\%$ and tin(II) fluoride $(\mathsf{S n F}_{2}$ , $99\%$ ) was purchased from Sigma Aldrich, USA. Cesium bromide (CsBr, $99.999\%$ , lead(II) bromide $(\mathsf{P b}\mathsf{B r}_{2}$ , $99.999\%$ , tin(II) iodide $(\mathsf{S n l}_{2}$ $,99.999\%)$ and anisole $(99\%)$ were purchased from Alfa Aesar, USA. Lead(II) iodide $(\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ , $99.99\%$ was purchased from Tokyo Chemical Industry, Japan. Formamidinium iodide (FAI, $99\%$ was purchased from Greatcell Solar Materials, Australia. Phenyl- ${\cdot}C_{61}$ -butyric acid methyl ester $(\mathsf{P C}_{67}\mathsf{B M}$ $99.5\%$ and bathocuproine (BCP, $99\%$ ) were purchased from OSM, Republic of Korea. Ethylene glycol (EG, $99.8\%$ , ethylenediamine (EDA, $99\%$ , dimethylformamide (DMF, $99.5\%)$ , dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO, $99.8\%$ , toluene $(99.8\%)$ and isopropyl alcohol (IPA, $99.5\%\AA$ were purchased from Samchun Chemical, Republic of Korea. Chlorobenzene (CBZ, $99.8\%$ was purchased from Kanto Chemical, Japan. Poly(3,4- ethylenedioxythiophene) polystyrenesulfonate (PEDOT:PSS, PVP AI 4083) was sourced from Clevios, Germany. ITO $(70~\Omega~\bigtriangledown^{-1})$ glass substrates were purchased from AMGlass, Republic of Korea.
Precursor Solution Preparation: The $1.0\cdot\mathsf{M}$ $\mathsf{F A}_{0.83}\mathsf{C s}_{0.17}\mathsf{P b}(\mathsf{I}_{0.83}\mathsf{B r}_{0.17})_{3}$ perovskite precursor solution was prepared by dissolving a molar ratio of FAI $(0.83~\mathsf{m})$ , $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ $(0.83~\mathsf{m})$ , $\mathsf{P b B r}_{2}$ $(0.77~\mathsf{M})$ , and CsBr $(0.77~\mathsf{M})$ in a mixed solvent of (4:1) DMF:DMSO. The lower concentration solution for $\mathsf{A V T}_{28.6}$ was prepared with 0.8-m $\mathsf{F A}_{0.83}\mathsf{C s}_{0.17}\mathsf{P b}(\mathsf{I}_{0.83}\mathsf{B r}_{0.17})_{3}$ to reduce film thickness. And the $0.8–\mathsf{M}\mathsf{F A}_{0.83}\mathsf{C s}_{0.17}\mathsf{P b}(\mathsf{I}_{0.66}\mathsf{B r}_{0.34})_{3}$ for increased bromine components solution for ${\sf A V T}_{35.3}$ was prepared by dissolving a molar ratio of FAI $(0.83~\mathsf{M})$ , $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ ( ${[0.575~\mathsf{m}}]$ ), $\mathsf{P b B r}_{2}~(0.425~\mathsf{M})$ , and CsBr $(0.77~\mathsf{M})$ in a mixed solvent of (4:1) DMF:DMSO in order to realize wider bandgap of the perovskite. The perovskite precursor solutions were dissolved using vortexing for $10\min$ .
Fabrication of Perovskite Solar Cells: The ITO substrates were cleaned sequentially with acetone and IPA by sonication for $30\mathrm{\min}$ in each step. After drying in an oven, the substrates were treated with ${\mathsf{U V}}{\cdot}{\mathsf{O}}_{3}$ treatment for 20 min before use. The nickel oxide $\left(\mathsf{N i O}_{x}\right)$ sol-gel solution was prepared to form the HTL; $\mathsf{l}_{-\mathsf{M}}\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ $6H_{2}O$ was dissolved in 1 mL EG with $50\upmu\up L$ EDA, and the solution was stirred for $2h$ before use. ${\mathsf{N i O}}_{x}$ sol-gel solution was spin-coated onto the substrates at 5000 rpm for $50~\mathsf{s}$ , and annealed at $300~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $60\ \mathrm{min}$ on a hot plate. Then, the samples were transferred into the nitrogen glove box to fabricate the perovskite layer. The perovskite precursor solution was spin-coated at 4000 rpm for $30~\mathsf{s}$ on top of the ${\mathsf{N i O}}_{x}$ film, treated with $300~\upmu\upiota$ anisole for 5 s before the end of spinning, and annealed at $125^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $20~\mathrm{min}$ on a hot plate. For the ETL, a $20\ m g\ m l^{-1}$ PCBM solution in CBZ was spincoated onto the perovskite film at 1500  rpm for $30~\mathsf{s}$ , and annealed at ${\mathsf{120}}\ {\mathsf{^{o}C}}$ for 5 min. Subsequently, a $0.5~\mathsf{m g}~\mathsf{m}^{\mathsf{I}-1}$ BCP solution in IPA was spin-coated onto the PCBM film at 4000 rpm for $20~\mathsf{s}$
Electrode Deposition: For the ST-PSCs, the transparent ITO and IO:GT were deposited by conventional 4  inch linear-facing target sputtering (LFTS). The sputtering process conditions for the $340\mathsf{n m}$ ITO electrodes were a working pressure of 3  mTorr, a gas flow rate for ${\sf A r}/{\sf O}_{2}$ of $20/0.2$ sccm, and a DC power of 100 W. To fabricate amorphous IO:GT electrodes, 1 wt. $\%$ Ga and 1 wt. $\%$ Ti co-doped $\mathsf{I n}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}$ targets were used during the LFTS process. To prepare the IO:GT electrodes, sputtering was performed under a base pressure of $2.0\times10^{-6}$ Torr, a $9c m$ target-to-target distance, a $6c m$ target-to-substrate distance, working pressure of 3.7  mTorr, a gas flow rate for $\mathsf{A r/O}_{2}$ of $10/0.3~\mathsf{s c c m}$ , and a sputtering power of $\mathsf{l}00\ \mathsf{W}$ at room temperature. In the case of opaque devices, $\mathsf{A g}$ electrodes were thermally evaporated under a high vacuum $(<]\times70^{-6}$ Torr) with a shadow mask $(4~\mathsf{m}\mathsf{m}^{2})$ .
Fabrication of Low Band Gap Perovskite Solar Cells: The low band gap $1.2\substack{-\mathsf{M}\mathsf{F A}_{0.83}\mathsf{C s}_{0.17}\mathsf{S}\mathsf{n}_{0.5}\mathsf{P b}_{0.5}\mathsf{I}_{3}}$ perovskite precursor solution was prepared by dissolving a molar ratio of FAI $(0.83~\mathsf{M})$ , CsI $(0.77~\mathsf{M})$ , $\mathsf{P b l}_{2}$ $(0.5~\mathsf{m})$ and $\mathsf{S n l}_{2}$ $(0.5~\mathsf{m})$ in a mixed solvent of (4:1) DMF:DMSO with $\mathsf{S n F}_{2}$ $(0.06~\mathsf{M})$ additive. PEDOT:PSS was spin-coated onto the cleaned ITO substrates at 5000 rpm for $50~\mathsf{s}$ in atmosphere condition, and annealed at ${150~^{\circ}C}$ for 20 min on a hotplate as a HTL. The PEDOT:PSS coated ITO substrates were transferred into the glove box for coating the low band gap perovskite layer. The perovskite precursor solution was spincoated at 5000 rpm for $30~\mathsf{s}$ . A $0.3~\mathsf{m L}$ of $\mathsf{P C}_{67}\mathsf{B M}$ solution dissolved in toluene $(7~\mathrm{mg}~\mathsf{m}\mathsf{L}^{-1})$ ) was applied drop-wise in the sample during the spin-coating. The perovskite coated substrate was annealed at $65~^{\circ}C$ for 1  min, sequentially annealed at $100~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $30{\mathsf{m i n}}$ . For an ETL, a $\mathsf{P C}_{67}\mathsf{B M}$ solution with $20\ m g\ m L^{-1}$ dissolved in CBZ was spin-coated onto the perovskite film at 1500  rpm for $35\mathrm{~s~}$ and annealed at $80~^{\circ}C$ for $10\mathrm{\min}$ . A BCP solution $(0.5~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{mL^{-1}}$ dissolved in IPA) was spincoated at 4000  rpm for $20\textup{s}$ without any post-annealing. The $\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}$ top electrode was thermally evaporated under a high vacuum condition $(<]\times10^{-6}$ Torr) with a shadow mask (the size of each solar cell device is $4\:\mathsf{m m}^{2}\}$ .
Characterization: All device performance characterizations were conducted in an ambient atmosphere, and the reflectance was reduced by applying commercial antireflective film (DayVue, NuShield). Current– voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ curves of the fabricated PSCs were measured using a solar simulator (PEC-L01, Peccell Technologies) under standard AM1.5 illumination $(700m\times)\times(m^{-2})$ . To detect the responses as a function of the spectral wavelength, the external quantum efficiency (EQE) spectrum was characterized using a power source (Abet Technologies 150 W xenon lamp, 13014) with a monochromator (DongWoo Optron, MonoRa500i) and a CompactStat instrument (Ivium Technologies). Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements for Nyquist plots, bode plots and Mott-Schottky plots were also conducted on the CompactStat equipment under zero bias and dark conditions at the frequency ranges of $2M H z$ to ${\mathsf{10}}~{\mathsf{H z}}$ . TR-PL was measured using Steady-State and Lifetime Spectro fluorometers (Fluoromax-4, Horiba). The analysis for perovskite damaged by sputtering was conducted using a Field emission transmission electron microscope (FE-TEM, JEM-F200, Jeol) and X-ray diffraction (XRD, D8-Advance, Bruker). The transmittance and absorbance (Tauc-plot) spectra were acquired using UV–vis spectrometry (UV-2700, Shimadzu). The electrical properties were measured using Hall effect measurement (HMS-4500, Ecopia) with a magnetic field of $0.52~\mathsf{T}$ and an operating current of $5~\mathsf{m A}$ . The work function was determined by ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS, Nexsa XPS system, Thermo Fisher Scientific) using a He I UV source $(27.2\ \mathrm{eV})$ .
Statistical Analysis: The provided UPS, TPC, durability test data were normalized. The statistical data presented are composed of 10 samples, and the calculated mean value and standard deviation values are shown in each table.
